 To apply a Gaussian filter before the Sobel edge detection in MATLAB, you need to add the gaussian filtering step before the application of the Sobel operators. Here's how you can modify your existing code:
```matlab
% Read input image
input_image = imread('image.jpg'); % Read input image
% Convert to grayscale
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image); % Convert to grayscale
% Define Gaussian filter (standard deviation = 1)
gaussian_filter = fspecial('gaussian', [3 3], 1);
% Apply Gaussian filter
filtered_image = imfilter(double(gray_image), gaussian_filter, 'same');
% Define Sobel operator in x direction
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
% Define Sobel operator in y direction
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];
% Apply Sobel operator in x direction
edge_image_x = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');
% Apply Sobel operator in y direction
edge_image_y = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');
% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
% Display original image, filtered image, and edge image
subplot(1,3,1), imshow(input_image), title('Original Image');
subplot(1,3,2), imshow(filtered_image), title('Filtered Image');
subplot(1,3,3), imshow(edge_image), title('Edge Image');
```
In the above code, we first define a Gaussian filter using the `fspecial()` function and then apply it to the grayscale image using the `imfilter()` function. The resulting filtered image is then used for edge detection using the Sobel operator. This approach helps reduce the noise in the image by smoothing it out with the Gaussian filter before detecting edges.

