 To retrieve the username from a JSON Web Token (JWT) in an ASP.NET C# WebApi, you'll first need to verify the token and then parse its claims. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Install `Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens` NuGet package if not already installed:
   ```
   Install-Package Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens
   ```

2. Create a method for JWT validation:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
using Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens;

public static bool ValidateToken(string token)
{
    try
    {
        // Set your secret key as a byte array
        var key = Encoding.ASCII.GetBytes("your_secret_key");

        // Parse the token
        var handler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
        var jwtToken = handler.ReadJwtToken(token);

        // Validate the token
        var validationParameters = new TokenValidationParameters
        {
            ValidateIssuerSigningKey = true,
            IssuerSigningKey = new SymmetricSecurityKey(key),
            ValidateIssuer = false,
            ValidateAudience = false,
            ClockSkew = TimeSpan.Zero
        };

        handler.ValidateToken(token, validationParameters, out _);

        return true;
    }
    catch (Exception)
    {
        return false;
    }
}
```
Replace `"your_secret_key"` with your actual secret key.

3. Create a method for getting the username from the validated token:

```csharp
public static string GetUsernameFromToken(string token)
{
    if (!ValidateToken(token)) throw new ArgumentException("Invalid token.");

    var handler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
    var jwtToken = handler.ReadJwtToken(token);

    return jwtToken.Claims.FirstOrDefault(x => x.Type == "sub")?.Value;
}
```

4. Usage example:

```csharp
string token = Request.Headers["Authorization"].ToString().Split(' ')[1]; // Bearer <token

