 To create a script for Unity that takes an audio file as input and displays a visualizer in sync with the music, you can use Unity's built-in `AudioSource` and `ParticleSystem` components along with some custom scripts. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Create a new C# script named `AudioVisualizer`. Replace its content with the following code:

```csharp
using System.Collections;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using UnityEngine;

public class AudioVisualizer : MonoBehaviour
{
    public GameObject particleSystemPrefab; // Drag & drop your ParticleSystem prefab here
    public AudioSource audioSource;

    private float[] samples = new float[64];
    private ParticleSystem particleSystem;

    void Start()
    {
        if (particleSystemPrefab == null) return;
        particleSystem = Instantiate(particleSystemPrefab).GetComponent<ParticleSystem>();
        Destroy(gameObject.GetComponent<MeshFilter>()); // Remove mesh to allow ParticleSystem to be parented
        particleSystem.transform.parent = transform;
    }

    void Update()
    {
        if (!audioSource.isPlaying) return;

        int samplesSize = audioSource.GetSpectrumData(samples, 0, FFTWindow.BlackmanHarris);

        if (particleSystem != null)
            particleSystem.Emit(samplesSize);
    }
}
```

2. Create a new GameObject in your scene and attach the `AudioVisualizer` script to it. Set the `AudioSource` component's `clip` field to your desired audio file.

3. Create a new ParticleSystem prefab by right-clicking in the Project window, selecting `Particle System`, and saving it as a new asset. Edit the settings of the particle system to create the desired visual effects.

4. In the `AudioVisualizer` script, drag and drop the created ParticleSystem prefab into the `particleSystemPrefab` field.

5. Add an `AudioSource` component to the GameObject with the `AudioVisualizer` script attached. Assign the same audio file to this `AudioSource`.

